      "reason": "The first obstruction is the claimed covolume comparison. The sketch gives no construction of a cyclotomic tower, thick places, or theta lifts with the stated witnesses, and gives no upper or product-formula lower bound for a common covolume. In particular, there is no estimate yielding the displayed prime-sum inequality for the same witnesses at every u in the prescribed interval. The allowed criterion takes a prime-error bound as a hypothesis and does not supply this comparison."
    },
    {
      "number": 2,
      "status": "proved",
      "reason": "Fix d and N. First, the logarithmic derivatives used in C are regular at 1 after the stated pole removal. To see this for any modulus K, a nonprincipal character has bounded partial sums, so summation by parts continues its Dirichlet series holomorphically to Re s>0. The principal L-function is ζ(s) times the finite factors for primes dividing K. Subtracting the integral of v^(−s) on [1,∞) from the zeta series continues ζ(s) to Re s>0 with a simple pole at 1, since the sum of the differences between each summand and its integral on [n,n+1] converges locally uniformly there. If any nonprincipal L-function vanished at 1, the product over all h=φ(K) characters would therefore be holomorphic throughout Re s>0. For Re s>1, its Euler factor at p∤K is (1−p^(−rs))^(−h/r), where r is the order of p in the unit group. Indeed, character values at p run through the r-th roots of unity equally often, as follows by summing their powers by character orthogonality. Thus the product has a Dirichlet series with nonnegative coefficients and coefficient at least 1 at every n^h with gcd(n,K)=1. In the Taylor series at s=2 evaluated at s=1/h, termwise differentiation of the Dirichlet series gives nonnegative terms. Interchanging the sums shows that the Taylor sum equals the Dirichlet series evaluated at 1/h. The Taylor series converges because 2−1/h<2, whereas the Dirichlet series dominates the divergent sum of 1/n over n coprime to K. This contradiction proves the required nonvanishing at 1.\n\nFor K=N and K=1, put A_K(v)=R_{d,K}(e^v) for v≥0 and A_K(v)=0 for v<0. The bound Λ(n)≤log n gives A_K(v)=O(1+v²) on v≥0. Let F_K(z)=∫₀^∞ A_K(v)e^(−zv) dv for Re z>0. Integrating the summand from log n to infinity gives n^(−1−z)/(z(z+1)). By character orthogonality and the Euler products, F_K(z)=D_K(1+z)/(z(z+1))−1/(φ(K)z²)−C_{d,K}/z, where D_K(s)=φ(K)^(−1)∑_{η mod K}(L′_{ηκ_d}/L_{ηκ_d}−L′_η/L_η)(s). Each twist is nonprincipal, since the Chinese remainder theorem gives a unit that is 1 modulo K and a nonsquare modulo d. Consequently D_K(1+z)=1/(φ(K)z)+B_K+O(z), where B_K is the average of the differences of the regular logarithmic derivatives. This constant is real because D_K is real for real s>1 by its Dirichlet series. The definition gives C_{d,K}=B_K−1/φ(K), so both poles in F_K cancel and F_K extends holomorphically to a neighborhood of 0.\n\nTake U=t²/2 and let G_t be the Gaussian probability density of mean U and variance t². For σ=t^(−2), the Gaussian Fourier integral and absolute integration give ∫ℝ A_K(v)G_t(v) dv=(2πi)^(−1)∫_{σ−i∞}^{σ+i∞} F_K(z)exp(zU+t²z²/2) dz. Choose fixed r>0 and 0<δ<min(r/2,1/4) so that the rectangle from −δ to σ with imaginary parts in [−r,r] lies in the neighborhood of holomorphy for both K and all large t. On the original line F_K is O(t⁶) by the growth bound, so the portions with |Im z|≥r are exponentially small in t². Shift the remaining segment to Re z=−δ. There the real part of the exponent is at most t²(−δ+δ²)/2. On the horizontal segments it is at most 1/2−3r²t²/8 for large t, using |Re z|≤r/2. The shifted integrals are therefore O(exp(−k t²)) for some k>0 after absorbing polynomial factors. For large t the complement of [t,t²] is at distance at least t²/4 from U. The Gaussian bound and A_K(v)=O(1+v²) show that restricting the original average to [t,t²] changes it by O(exp(−k′t²)) for some k′>0, by retaining half the Gaussian exponent to integrate the polynomial bound. The interval has Gaussian mass at least 1/2 for large t. Uniformly for 0≤c≤e^t, the restricted average of A_N−cA_1 has absolute value O((1+e^t)exp(−min(k,k′)t²)), which is less than e^(−t)/2 eventually. If A_N(u)−cA_1(u)>e^(−t) at every point of the interval, the average would exceed e^(−t)/2. This proves the claimed existence of u with the required uniform threshold in c."
    }
  ],
  "parent_closes": false,
  "parent_reason": "Gap 1 is unproved. If its witnesses existed, the stated numerical deduction would work for the displayed error R. The class a=M/2−1 is a unit since any common divisor of a and M divides 2 and a is odd. It is 7 modulo 8, and each prime d in the class satisfies M∣2(d+1) and M∤d+1. The choice of ε gives 0<ε≤1/102 and 1/2<β<Re ρ. For each such d and all sufficiently large x, c=T/H≤log b=x^ε≤e^t for t=10^6 log x. Gap 2 then supplies a u covered by Gap 1. Dividing by maH gives R_{d,M}(x)≤φ(M)x^(−1/2+6ε)+x^(−10^6). Dividing this upper bound by x^(β−1) gives φ(M)x^(−2ε)+x^(−10^6+1/2−8ε), which tends to zero. This is eventual for each d separately, as the criterion requires. The criterion is phrased in terms of primeError, so its application also requires identifying that quantity with R_{d,M}, an equality the supplied text leaves implicit. With that identification and Gap 1, the criterion would yield nonvanishing for the induced character at ρ, and the allowed Euler-factor comparison would transfer it to χ away from the stated principal exception."
